<TopicPage slug="subjects/astronomy/intermediate/the-milky-way">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The Milky Way is our city: <strong>a hundred billion stars in a barred spiral 100,000
    light-years across</strong>, with the Sun in a minor arm, two-thirds of the way out. We live
    inside it, edge-on — which is why it appears as a hazy band in dark skies and why mapping it
    took until the 20th century (Harlow Shapley, 1920, found our off-centre position by tracking
    globular clusters).
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="four facts about the galaxy we live inside of" widget="equationStep" config={milkyCfg} />

  <h2>How we know our own shape</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Radio eyes were essential:</strong> dust hides the centre from optical light; 21-cm radio emission from hydrogen mapped the spiral arms in the 1950s.</li>
    <li><strong>Gaia (2013–) is mapping it properly:</strong> over a billion stars with positions, distances and motions — discovering that the Milky Way is still digesting smaller galaxies it has swallowed, their star streams visible as ripples.</li>
    <li><strong>We are heading for a merger:</strong> the Andromeda galaxy approaches at 110 km/s and will collide with the Milky Way in ~4.5 billion years — stars themselves almost never collide, but the galaxies will braid into one elliptical.</li>
    <li><strong>Perspective check:</strong> every star you can see unaided is in the Milky Way. "Galaxy" comes from the Greek for milk — the band was our ancestors' spill of the goddess Hera's.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> ESA Gaia mission releases; Genzel et al. (Nobel 2020); OpenStax, Astronomy 2e (The Milky Way Galaxy).</p>
</TopicPage>
